\f0\fs24 \cf2 <$Scr_Ps::0>
\f1\fs22 Chanta showered for much longer than it took to wash the old drunk off, longer than it took to slake away his hangover, longer than it took to clean his fingernails whiter than they\'92d been in years. The shower was the Goldilocks of showers\'97perfect heat, perfect pressure, perfect everything. Even the sound of the water had a jazz drummer\'92s pizzazz; the drain warbled a blues spiritual.\
\pard\pardeftab720\fi360\sl220\qj\partightenfactor0

\f0\fs24 \cf2 \expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable><!$Scr_Ps::0><$Scr_Ps::1>
\f1\fs22 Pink and puffy, Chanta dried himself on a towel as thick as ceiling insulation, wrapped it around his waist, and sauntered into the adjoining change room. A stack of boxes sat on a bench, looking like they might hold doughnuts\'97dozens of them. Chanta, craving fat and sugar post-hangover, was disappointed to find clothes instead: sneakers, socks, underwear, jeans, T-shirt, and a light jumper (Chanta refused to call them sweaters). As he dressed, he couldn\'92t shake the feeling he could buy several karaoke bars with the cost of the socks.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sl220\sb220\qj\partightenfactor0

\f0\fs24 \cf2 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 <!$Scr_Ps::1><$Scr_Ps::0>
\f1\fs22 \'93What\'92s all this?\'94 Chanta asked Fisher of the line of Elvi standing at ease in various kinds of uniforms, along the threshold to the reception room. Having stopped counting after the four nearest Elvi, Chanta estimated there were a dozen of them. Whatever their number, Chanta grinned. Mostly Elvines. That made sense; Elvines were more reliable.\
\pard\pardeftab720\fi360\sl220\qj\partightenfactor0

\f0\fs24 \cf2 \expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable><!$Scr_Ps::0><$Scr_Ps::1>
\f1\fs22 \'93These are your senior staff and immediate assistants.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93My staff\'85\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Jamison Hardy always had a soft spot for Grayling Corporation units, sir,\'94 Fisher explained.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Um, me too,\'94 Chanta answered, distracted by the reference to Grayling Corporation.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93That is splendid news.\'94 Fisher smiled professionally and with all the charm he could muster which, being an ELVIS, was prodigious. \'93May I present your personal assistant, Greg Nolan.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Chanta took the Elvine\'92s proffered hand and gently shook it.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Short for Gregoria,\'94 Greg explained.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Chanta nodded, for a moment in his element. \'93Yes. I am familiar with Elvi naming conventions. I look forward to you helping me live a little.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93And love a little?\'94 Greg blushed.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Fisher coughed politely into his gloved hand. \'93And this is Ricki Richards, your chauffeur.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Chanta shook Ricki\'92s hand, suddenly aware of how formal this all was.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>And so they went down the line: Rusty Wells, Head of Entertainment and Hospitality; Jessica Wade, Head of Security; Tobi Kwimper, Head of Housekeeping; Rosalind Carpenter, Executive Chef; Ross Carpenter, Sommelier.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Is that like a brigadier?\'94 Chanta asked earnestly.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Ross Carpenter explained his role.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Chanta doubted he\'92d need a summer liar, and asked, \'93What should I drink with a cheeseburger and fries?\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>The sommelier looked Chanta up and down. \'93Kenyan lager.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Chanta doubted there was such a thing as Kenyan lager, but was impressed with Ross\'92s answer. Perhaps he\'92d keep him on after all. Perhaps he\'92d keep them all on.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93I\'92m sure you\'92ll meet the rest in good time,\'94 Fisher said.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93The rest?\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Fisher hesitated, then nodded. \'93Yes, sir, the rest of the staff.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Chanta wondered how many there were, all of them \'85 then it struck him: the staff he\'92d met were acting very strange toward him\'97it all felt a bit fake.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Will there be anything else, sir?\'94 Fisher asked.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Why aren\'92t you calling me Great Teacher?\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Is it what you wish?\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Chanta didn\'92t want to sound demanding, though he was feeling an unfamiliar dollop of indignation on top of the ice cream sundae of his emotions. It was his right to be called Great Teacher among the Elvi. He\'92d earned it. And earned the Elvi quite a groat from record sales and tourism. Which reminded him, when was he getting his share of the pie? \'93Yes. Yes, it is my wish.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Very good.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Chanta raised his ever more privileged eyebrow. \'93Well, in any case, tell the staff not to rush,\'94 he said, reclaiming his empathy. \'93I know public transport from the Ghetto is unreliable at best.\'94 Chanta\'92s brain made a thought. \'93How do they get through the front door?\'94 He shuddered at an involuntary vision of the drunk sliced in half.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93We keep all our staff on site, sir. Great Teacher.\'94 Fisher was matter-of-fact. The line of Elvi tried to keep their feet from shuffling. \'93Is it not the same with your other ELVIS units?\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93My ELVIS units? They\'92re my friends.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Feet-shuffling became irresistible.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93You are friends with your entertainment and hospitality units?\'94 the sommelier asked.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Well, the Airborne look down upon any Cloudborne with disdain, but\'97\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93What do the Cloudborne have to do with ELVIS units?\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Chanta was completely bewildered. \'93The Elvi are considered Cloudborne,\'94 he explained, disbelieving with every syllable that an explanation was necessary.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Fisher straightened and nodded like a toy soldier. \'93Of course, Great Teacher,\'94 he said, clearly revising his mental model as he spoke. \'93The Cloudborne, like ELVIS units, are property, mere chattels, for the Airborne to use as they see fit.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93What the tax are you saying, Fisher? People aren\'92t units. Cloudborne aren\'92t property. And neither are the Elvi.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Well, I\'92ve never heard of such a thing,\'94 Fisher muttered while maintaining a stiff lip. It was clear from the expression of the other staff, neither had they.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93You\'92ve never heard of the emancipation of the Elvi? You were set free generations ago.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Chanta was met with stunned Elvi silence, until Fisher uttered, \'93You are indeed the Great Teacher. And it seems we have been in stasis for much longer than we might have ever guessed.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93You\'92ve been in stasis since\'85\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Since the death of the Old Man. Jamison Hardy.\'94 Fisher gulped like a fish, like he was gulping on behalf of the whole staff.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>It took more than a moment for Chanta to think. He looked down at his new white sneakers, sunk into the thick carpet, and something about how clean they were made everything worse.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93The rest of the staff! Right now. Where are they?\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93In the bunker, Great Teacher.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93The bunker\'85\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Fisher appeared embarrassed. \'93Yes, well, when Starborne One was on the drawing board, it was quite fashionable for billionaires to buy up underground fallout shelters. The Old Man himself owned several, scattered around the country and the world.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93How do you fall out of underground shelters?\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Fisher chortled. \'93Indeed, Great Teacher\'85 oh, you are serious.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Have you got them locked up down there?\'94 Chanta had been locked up more than once, always unfairly.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Not at all, Great Teacher. They are on the bunker\'92s stasis level.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93We have to get them out of stasis. Right now.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93I\'92ll see to it immediately.\'94 Fisher marched towards the elevator lobby.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93I\'92m coming with you.\'94 Chanta said as he pulled his tablet from his pocket and called a voice link.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>The other end rang once then redirected. A recorded message said: \

\f2\i <$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>You\'92ve reached the office of the High Elvis. Are you lonesome tonight?
